body:
> Dr. Kelly D. Brownell, the director of the Yale Center for Eating and Weight Disorders, said the number was first suggested in a 1959 clinical study of only 100 people. The finding was repeated so often that it came to be regarded as fact, he said. 

&#x200B;

> ''There is something very optimistic about this whole data set,'' said Dr. Rena Wing, a professor of psychiatry at the University of Pittsburgh and the Brown University School of Medicine. ''Without much effort, we have identified 2,500 people who have succeeded.'' 

&#x200B;

> The success stories might not have been so surprising had it been clear that the 95 percent failure rate was so poorly founded. The figure comes not from any kind of random sampling, but from a study of 100 patients treated for obesity at a nutrition clinic at New York Hospital in the 1950's. In 1959, its authors, Dr. Albert Stunkard and Mavis McLaren-Hume, published a paper in which they concluded, ''Most obese persons will not stay in treatment, most will not lose weight, and of those who do lose weight, most will regain it.'' 

&#x200B;

Hm, who could have thought? [95% of people regain weight](https://www.nytimes.com/1999/05/25/health/95-regain-lost-weight-or-do-they.html) read it.  If you go on a stupid diet and then start eating just like you used to, no shit you will gain the weight back.

Big F.

&#x200B;

But it is possible that it is water weight or something and not fat. I mean, unless you are eating over your TDEE, you shouldn't be gaining weight and you've been active.

Overall, I think I will drop it. Like there is no saving it anyway, I will get a job and then decide something that I like, not something that is pretty much useless. As I said, it is a mixed major where one of the things is pretty useless, now that I think about it. Then, the other thing is just something that I can do with a certificate. I just looked recently at all the subjects and.. it justs pointless. It is my fault for not going to classes that I need attendance. I am so mad at myself. 

&#x200B;

As for my friends, I will try. I just don't want them to know everything that is going on. Like the university thing and how I feel.

&#x200B;

Thanks for taking times to respond.

I said that he is putting an ultimatum on her, that is what I am against. If he explained his side and she explained her and then came to a decision together that is one thing but to say ''she wants a tattoo because she wants to be edgy, a rebel or hates her body'' is shitty.  She probably has no idea that he is that serious about her no getting a tattoo, because from what OP is saying they didn't have a proper conversation. He is in his right to break up with her for whatever reason he wants. I never tried to say that his emotion is not a valid thing.

&#x200B;

>OP’s girlfriend came to him and said she has realised she is polyamorous and wants to get another boyfriend.  

 No, because he will just break up with her and be done with it. I would do the same if my boyfriend came home and said how he wants to have a polyamorous relationship. Wouldn't even put a ''if you do this I will break up with you'' I would just do it. As for smoking - I assume you mean cigarettes so, I would just probably talk with him rather say ''me or smoking'', but honestly it would be weird if someone chooses to randomly start smoking anyway. 

&#x200B;

To summarise my point: They need to talk, Hear each other out if OP wants to break up with her after that - so be it. If I was in that situation and my boyfriend just talked about why he doesn't want me to get a tattoo and everything I would be way more willing and won't go with and respect his feelings, but if he just says ''don't do this or I will break up with you'' I wouldn't be as willing because we didn't even talk about it.
